  "worked_solution": "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14094599/assets/169844e6d45393119ac1579cc89e34850401810bfcc1c7537d5b72851a0b2b02.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,\u03a9.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n12=40I+200q+20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(12) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(40I+200q+20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =40\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=0.\n$$",
